<article class="detail">
  <h1>What is Object Biography?</h1>
  <div class="detail-text">
    <p>Every object has a history.</p>

    <p>Object Biography is an independent project exploring military artifacts and the people who used them.</p>

    <p>I&rsquo;ve collected militaria for over 30 years, with a particular interest in objects that retain a connection to their original owners. Through photographs, firsthand accounts, and historical research, I document the details and personal histories that might otherwise be lost.</p>

    <p>A collector preserves the object. A historian preserves its meaning. Object Biography is my effort to do both.</p>

    <p>Not every story can be fully told, nor is every detail meant to be shared. My goal is to preserve what can be established, respect the people behind these objects, and share what makes them worth remembering.</p>

    <h2 id="how-these-histories-are-handled">How These Histories Are Handled</h2>

    <p>Many of the objects here came to me directly from veterans or their families, and their accounts and wishes guide how those pages are written.</p>

    <p>Others were acquired through dealers, auctions, and the secondhand market, where the veteran could not be consulted. For those, I limit research to public and archival records and leave out sensitive personal details.</p>

    <p>Private individuals in an object&rsquo;s history are described by their role, not by name, unless they have agreed to be identified.</p>

    <p>If you are a veteran, a family member, or someone who knew the person behind an object, I would be glad to hear from you. Corrections and additional information are always welcome. At the request of a veteran or their immediate family, I will remove a name or personal details from a page. <a class="accent-link" href="/contact/">Get in touch.</a></p>

    <blockquote class="hero-quote page-footnote">
      <p>&ldquo;Biographies of things can make salient what might otherwise remain obscure.&rdquo;</p>
      <cite>&mdash; Igor Kopytoff, <em>The Cultural Biography of Things</em> (1986)</cite>
    </blockquote>
  </div>
</article>
